{:edb/db-before-t 1893 :edb/db-after-t 1894 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535206 50 #inst "2026-10-13T03:36:54.521Z" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186053727 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053728 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186053727 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053729 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186053727 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053731 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186053727 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053732 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186053727 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479366 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186053727 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479381 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186053727 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479557 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186053727 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479721 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186053727 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479381 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054381 1071 "Find the value of $q$ that satisfies the equation $\\dfrac{q}{4}=-9$." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054381 1071 "Find the value of $q$ that satisfies the equation $\\dfrac{q}{4}=-9.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054381 1074 "In the equation, $q$ is being divided by $4$. To isolate $q$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $4$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4$.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{q}{4} &=-9 \\\\[5pt] 4\\cdot \\dfrac{q}{4} &=4\\cdot \\left(-9\\right) \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{4q}{4} &= -36 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{4q}{4} &= -36 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\cancel{4}q}{\\cancel{4}} &= -36 \\\\ q &=-36 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054381 1074 "In the equation, $q$ is being divided by $4.$ To isolate $q$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $4$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{q}{4} &=-9\\[5pt]\n\t\t4\\cdot \\dfrac{q}{4} &=4\\cdot \\left(-9\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{4q}{4} &= -36\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{4q}{4} &= -36 \\[5pt]\n                \\dfrac{\\cancel{4}q}{\\cancel{4}} &= -36 \\\n\t\tq &=-36\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054382 1071 "Find the value of $k$ that satisfies the equation $-\\dfrac{k}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{3}$." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054382 1071 "Find the value of $k$ that satisfies the equation $-\\dfrac{k}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054382 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{k}{2} &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot (-\\frac{k}{2}) &= (-1) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n\\frac{k}{2} &= -\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $k$ is being divided by $2$. To isolate $k$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{k}{2} &=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[5pt] 2 \\cdot \\dfrac{k}{2} &=2 \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{2k}{2} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\cancel{2}k}{\\cancel{2}} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[5pt] k &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054382 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{k}{2}) & =(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $k$ is being divided by $2.$ To isolate $k$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{k}{2} &=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\[5pt]\n\t\t2 \\cdot \\dfrac{k}{2} &=2 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{2k}{2} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}k}{\\cancel{2}} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\[5pt]\n\t\tk &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054383 1071 "Solve the equation $24=-3z$." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054383 1071 "Solve the equation $24=-3z.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054383 1074 "First, let's write the equation in a way that's a little more familiar to us. We can do this by swapping the left and right-hand sides of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24 &= -3z \\\\\n- 3z &= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-3$. To isolate $z$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-3$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} -3z &=24 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {-3z} {-3} &= \\dfrac {24} {-3} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac {\\cancel{-3}z} {\\cancel{-3}} &= -8 \\\\[5pt] z &=-8 \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054383 1074 "First, let's write the equation in a way that's a little more familiar to us. We can do this by swapping the left and right-hand sides of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =-3z \\\\ -3z & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-3.$ To isolate $z$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-3$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t-3z &=24 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {-3z} {-3} &= \\dfrac {24} {-3} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {\\cancel{-3}z} {\\cancel{-3}} &= -8 \\[5pt]\n\t\tz &=-8\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054388 1071 "If $\\dfrac{1}{7}=\\dfrac{x}{2}$, then $x=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054388 1071 "If $\\dfrac{1}{7}=\\dfrac{x}{2},$ then $x=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054388 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $x$ is being divided by $2$. To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{2} &= \\frac{1}{7} \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{2} &= 2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{2}{7} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{2}{7} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{2}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054388 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $x$ is being divided by $2.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & =\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054495 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{3y}{y(y+1)}$." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054495 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{3y}{y(y+1)}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054495 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors.\n\n![](images/51/51bf9631f8b2ca90bda343a84a675f302ebae205446e528f5f05a8ea1f5f577b.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](images/f3/f3edb95bc0a0a57a6dc439a06e1653d95c23dcd49e701b4d4d3f3d8c0f36c594.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{3}{y+1}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054495 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors.\n\n$$\n(3y)/(y(y + 1)) = (3 \\cdot y)/(y \\cdot (y + 1))\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $y$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3 \\cdot y)/(y \\cdot (y + 1)) &=  \\\\\n(3 \\cdot y)/(y \\cdot (y + 1)) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{y + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{y+1}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054496 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{20}{8(z-1)}$." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054496 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{20}{8(z-1)}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054496 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $20$ in the numerator  becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5,$ and the $8(z-1)$ in the denominator becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z-1).$\n\n![](images/47/47eb2277b9892fbd83cb1ae58c1c2a7640c29326402970254fb119c8c6c9dd5f.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n![](images/28/28f5bd692129118ef790ef1dde224e18f0a70674bf6ccd19eed4dbdb78b7b1f6.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{5}{2(z-1)}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054496 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $20$ in the numerator becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5$, and the $8(z-1)$ in the denominator becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z-1)$.\n\n$$\n(20)/(8(z - 1)) = (2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5)/(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z - 1))\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5)/(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z - 1)) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5)/(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z - 1)) &=  \\\\\n(5)/(2 \\cdot (z - 1)) &=  \\\\\n&= (5)/(2(z - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2(z-1)}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054590 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{6y(x - 2)}{9yz}$." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054590 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{6y(x - 2)}{9yz}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054590 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6y(x-2)$ in the numerator  becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x-2),$ and the $9yz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z.$\n\n![](images/70/70068644b054adae3915b30b4c8fe3745190ec2a9eef2b1672528189e8dab9fd.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](images/38/382b839130e9e8b59572303be4644e8c45c2be55c4b9f45ff2bd664037eecf8e.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2(x - 2)}{3z}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054590 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6y(x-2)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x-2)$, and the $9yz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z$.\n\n$$\n(6y(x - 2))/(9yz) = (2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x - 2))/(3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z)\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x - 2))/(3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x - 2))/(3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot (x - 2))/(3z) &=  \\\\\n&= (2(x - 2))/(3z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2(x - 2)}{3z}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054591 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{6a(c+1)}{3b(c+1)}$." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054591 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{6a(c+1)}{3b(c+1)}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054591 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6a(c+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c+1)$, and the $3b(c+1)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot b \\cdot (c+1)$.\n\n$$\n(6a(c + 1))/(3b(c + 1)) = (2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c + 1))/(3 \\cdot b \\cdot (c + 1))\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(c+1)$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c + 1))/(3 \\cdot b \\cdot (c + 1)) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c + 1))/(3 \\cdot b \\cdot (c + 1)) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2 \\cdot a}{b} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{2a}{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2a}{b}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186054591 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6a(c+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c+1),$ and the $3b(c+1)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot b \\cdot (c+1).$\n\n![](images/7d/7d19b8a64cafa7f78bdf3795eba734282ad0a158b26ce12978f474aa12c84211.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(c+1)$'s.\n\n![](images/72/72b3b315f67d06181cc1457b16fcee0e886c224960030a5975723761513e2371.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2a}{b}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479894 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479901 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479837 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479885 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479846 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479861 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479868 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479873 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479878 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479884 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479855 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479893 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479828 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479865 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479831 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479882 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479844 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479891 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054901 1050 420.0 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186054920 1050 480.0 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186055415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186055415 1071 "$\\frac{10x}{12(x+1)}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186055415 1071 "What is $(10x)/(12(x + 1))$?" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186055415 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $10x$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 5\\cdot x,$ and the $12(x+1)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10x}{12(x+1)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186055937 1064 "If a rational expression is expressed in factored form, we can sometimes make the expression even simpler by pulling out factors that are common in both the numerator and denominator.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to simplify the rational expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4(2x-3)}{2}\n$$\n\nIf we decompose $4$ into $2\\cdot 2$, we can pull out a factor of $2$ from both the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4(2x - 3))/(2) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (2x - 3))/(2) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2} \\cdot (2 \\cdot (2x - 3))/(1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{2}\n$$\n\nis the same as $1$, and anything times $1$ is just itself, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{2} \\cdot (2 \\cdot (2x - 3))/(1) &=  \\\\\n1 \\cdot (2 \\cdot (2x - 3))/(1) &=  \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (2x - 3))/(1) \\\\\n&= 2(2x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** There is a shortcut! We can just \"cancel\" a $2$ from the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(4(2x - 3))/(2) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (2x - 3))/(2) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (2x - 3))/(2) &=  \\\\\n(2 \\cdot (2x - 3))/(1) &=  \\\\\n&= 2(2x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186055937 1064 "If a rational expression is expressed in factored form, we can sometimes make the expression even simpler by pulling out factors that are common in both the numerator and denominator.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to simplify the rational expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4(2x-3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we decompose $4$ into $2\\cdot 2,$ we can pull out a factor of $2$ from both the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fraction $\\dfrac{2}{2}$ is the same as $1,$ and anything times $1$ is just itself, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ 1\\cdot \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} \\\\ 2(2x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** There is a shortcut! We can just \"cancel\" a $2$ from the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2x-3)}{\\cancel{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot (2x-3)}{1} & = \\\\ 2(2x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186056009 1064 "Let's suppose that we want to solve an equation like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac x 6 = 4\n$$\n\nFor this, we can use the **multiplication principle.** The multiplication principle tells us that multiplying (or dividing) both sides of an equation by the same number does not change its solution.\n\nIn this case, the variable $x$ is being *divided* by $6$. To isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *multiplying* by $6$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac x 6 &= 4 \\\\[5pt] {\\color{blue}{6}} \\cdot \\dfrac x 6 &= {\\color{blue}{6}}\\cdot 4 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{6x}{6} &=24 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{6x}{6} &=24 \\ \\dfrac{\\cancel{6} x}{\\cancel{6}} &=24 \\\\ x &=24 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our solution is $x=24$. Let's check that this solution is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac x 6 &=4 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{24}{6} &=4 \\\\[5pt] 4 &= 4 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186056009 1064 "Let's suppose that we want to solve an equation like \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x 6 = 4.