 a positive. \n\nFor example, suppose that we have the equation\n\n$$\n-x = 3\n$$\n\nWe can isolate $x$ by multiplying both sides of the equation by $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x) & =(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the equation is $x=-3.$ Let's check to see that this works by substituting our solution back into the original equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =3 \\\\ -(-3) & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186056011 1064 "To solve an equation like $7x = 21$, we again need to look at what's happening to the variable and ask ourselves what the opposite operation is.\n\nIn this case, the variable $x$ is being *multiplied* by $7$. To isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $7$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $7$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7x &= 21 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{7x}{{\\color{blue}7}} &=\\dfrac{21}{{\\color{blue}7}} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{7x}{{\\color{blue}7}} &=\\dfrac{21}{{\\color{blue}7}} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}7}} &=3 \\\\[5pt] x &=3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check that our answer is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7x &=21 \\\\[5pt] 7(3) &=21 \\\\[5pt] 21 &= 21 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If there is a negative coefficient on a variable, we can get rid of it by dividing, like we do with positive coefficients. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2y &= 10 \\\\\n\\frac{-2y}{-2} &= \\frac{10}{-2} \\\\\n\\frac{-2y}{-2} &= -5 \\\\\ny &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186056011 1064 "To solve an equation like $7x = 21$, we again need to look at what's happening to the variable and ask ourselves what the opposite operation is.\n\