essions like $2^3, 2^2, 2^1$, and even $2^0.$ But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate $2^{-1},$ for example?\n\nTo help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {2}^{-1} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is just the reciprocal of $2.$ So, raising a number to the power of $-1$ is the *same* as finding its reciprocal!" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186087776 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479840 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186087777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479843 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102531 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102532 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102533 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102534 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102535 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479356 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479508 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479533 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479661 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186102530 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479733 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186102530 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479508 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186105730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479836 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186105730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479850 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186105957 1050 1080.0 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186106109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186106109 1071 "Consider the points $A(t, 2, 1)$, $B(2t, 3, 1)$ and $C(2, t^{2},-1)$. Find $∠BAC$ given that $AB^(→) \\cdot AC^(→) = 2$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186106109 1071 "Consider the points $A(t,2,1)$, $B(2t,3,1)$ and $C(2,{t}^{2},-1).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186106109 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨t,\\,2,\\,1⟩,\\,b=⟨2t,\\,3,\\,1⟩,\\,c=⟨2,\\,{t}^{2},\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3,\\,1⟩-⟨t,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,{t}^{2},\\,-1⟩-⟨t,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-t,\\,{t}^{2}-2,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =2 \\\\ t\\cdot (2-t)+1\\cdot ({t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (-2) & =2 \\\\ 2t-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}-2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2t-2 & =2 \\\\ 2t & =4 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,1,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨2-2,\\,{2}^{2}-2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})≈{71.57}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186107072 1064 "Consider the two vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-2, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 3,0,-1 \\rangle$ shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nTo find the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors, we can rearrange the dot product formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot |\\,b\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}(\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the magnitudes of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ |\\,b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\Bigg( \\dfrac{1}{3\\sqrt{10}} \\Bigg) \\approx 83.9^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186107072 1064 "Consider the two vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-2, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 3,0,-1 \\rangle$ shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nTo find the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors, we can rearrange the do