 [17592186124961 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},4t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186124961 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},4⟩ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{1}-4\\cdot 1)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186124999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122594 1