n and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{n - 2}{m - 1} + (2(m + 1))/(n) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{6 - 2}{4 - 1} + (2(4 + 1))/(6) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} + (2(5))/(6) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{10}{6} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{5}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{4 + 5}{3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{9}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186133495 1074 "We replace $m$ with $4$ and $n$ with $6$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{n-2}{m-1}+\\frac{2(m+1)}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{6-2}{4-1}+\\frac{2(4+1)}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{2(5)}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{10}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4+5}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{3} & = \\\\ 3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133582 1071 "Consider the vector ![](images/a1/a143c04731aab261451f29291a01d4d15cc42b6042d0df00a5e5a474a2ca1061.svg) Given that $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=-12$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v}\\Vert =4,$ what is the smallest possible value of $k?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133582 1071 "Consider the vector ![](images/d1/d1a095e61e6ea64f97a105fd69bc07f3f44990cd6d9794e54e355463c37a2413.png) Given that $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=-12$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v}\\Vert =4,$ what is the smallest possible value of $k?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186133583 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vectors ![](images/5e/5ed21f4bb536757026315cce838f4cc3028493adac2ff29d46a5b8cbdf11170b.png) and ![](images/8d/8d813e104a708be37ba44b9448806f49ea21a16fe7f613314d2eeab2b59bbda2.png)" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186133583 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vectors ![](images/a9/a90f3b03152a6e3dee2d0b4853fc411d946c67a374a9819bd3f5eaf8573db851.svg) and ![](images/7b/7b05ec66921317711d4ba676e77f567b18efe1580074ced7a73ce0fc0e3a2348.svg)" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479851 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479862 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479866 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479898 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479826 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479875 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479879 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479886 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479871 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133962 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479900 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133963 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479857 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479874 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133964 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479897 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479838 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479890 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186133988 1050 840.0 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186134036 1050 600.0 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186134218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186134218 1071 "Given that $‖u‖ = 4$ and $u \\cdot v =-16$, find the smallest possible value of $‖2v‖$." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186134218 1071 "Given that $‖u‖=4$ and $u\\cdot v=-16$, find the smallest possible value of $‖2v‖.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186134218 1074 "Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-16| & \\le 4\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the inequality by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 4 & \\le 2\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 8 & \\le ‖2v‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖2v‖$ is $8.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186135038 1064 "The **Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality** (sometimes called the **Cauchy-Bunyakovsky-Schwarz Inequality**) provides a connection between dot products and norms. It states that for any two vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$, we have the inequality\n\n$$\n\\vert \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the absolute value of the dot product is no more than the product of the norms.\n\nFor instance, if ![](images/6f/6f13203f5c0bc85b3af24b0a276e30044825c0133a6e8b42e6500cb20c0e754c.svg) and ![](images/80/809bfd2b9daeb6f8eee30bbe9ddbfe996326dcf1954cd3f9e09e072e95a16aa3.svg) then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |3\\cdot 5+(-4)\\cdot 12| & \\le \\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\ |-33| & \\le 5\\cdot 13 \\\\ 33 & \\le 65.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** An easy trick to remember the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality is to think of the formula for the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u \\cdot \\mathbf v = \\Vert \\mathbf u \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf v \\Vert \\cos \\theta\n$$\n\nBecause $\\vert \\cos \\theta \\vert \\leq 1,$ we can see that\n\n$$\n\\vert \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The above trick is only a mnemonic trick, *not* an actual proof of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. The formula $\\mathbf u \\cdot \\mathbf v = \\Vert \\mathbf u \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf v \\Vert \\cos \\theta$ is actually a *consequence* of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, so a proof of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality cannot use the formula." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186135038 1064 "The **Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality** (sometimes called the **Cauchy-Bunyakovsky-Schwarz Inequality**) provides a connection between dot products and norms. It states that for any two vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$, we have the inequality\n\n$$\n\\vert \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the absolute value of the dot product is no more than the product of the norms.\n\nFor instance, if $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |3\\cdot 5+(-4)\\cdot 12| & \\le \\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\ |-33| & \\le 5\\cdot 13 \\\\ 33 & \\le 65.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** An easy trick to remember the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality is to think of the formula for the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u \\cdot \\mathbf v = \\Vert \\mathbf u \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf v \\Vert \\cos \\theta\n$$\n\nBecause $\\vert \\cos \\theta \\vert \\leq 1,$ we can see that\n\n$$\n\\vert \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The above trick is only a mnemonic trick, *not* an actual proof of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. The formula $\\mathbf u \\cdot \\mathbf v = \\Vert \\mathbf u \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf v \\Vert \\cos \\theta$ is actually a *consequence* of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, so a proof of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality cannot use the formula." 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186135171 1064 "A **rational expression** is, roughly speaking, an algebraic expression that's in the form of a fraction, or contains fractions.\n\nTo evaluate a rational expression like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 6 x\n$$\n\nfor $x=2$, all we need to do is replace $x$ with $2$ in the above expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{x} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186135171 1064 "A **rational expression** is, roughly speaking, an algebraic expression that's in the form of a fraction, or contains fractions.\n\nTo evaluate a rational expression like $\\dfrac 6 x$ for $x=2,$ all we need to do is replace $x$ with $2$ in the above expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{x}=\\frac{6}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186192765 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479858 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186192765 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479881 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186198012 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](images/26/2614cbd879ea1014caf69d1239b69e81ebefcc76b8946962092cb83cc96dc987.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 false] [17592186198012 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](images/b5/b52614b0fbaccee13747bedb98617811af7a4b13e64e14f761a16e6b274513ed.svg)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186198012 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/5e/5e8faca7bd98b1af626603c26aef5fc4d7a65064f4cdc3486670195fc29dff65.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186198012 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/9b/9b0ee6271962e3ede406351f05d805ae60f4b37ce5a45489b117ab3786ed3c23.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compu