045417 true] [17592186479824 1079 #uuid "fe3e14eb-22b8-5147-9028-3893a72c3d27" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479355 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479610 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479679 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479686 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479689 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1077 "selection" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479686 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479825 1079 #uuid "ff4a0306-bebb-5a0b-88e8-580bcd8a3741" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479785 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479826 1069 "q-40883" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479826 1071 "Given that $‖u‖=5$ and $u\\cdot v=-30$, find the smallest possible value of $‖3v‖.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479826 1072 #uuid "0502cc65-db2e-5570-9171-0d7053693770" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479826 1074 "Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-30| & \\le 5\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 6 & \\le ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the inequality by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 6 & \\le 3\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ 18 & \\le ‖3v‖\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖3v‖$ is $18.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479752 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479827 1069 "q-69431" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479827 1071 "What is $(-2)^{-1}?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479827 1072 #uuid "05d822fa-9466-542c-b991-b0e81032fa5c" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479827 1074 "The expression $(-2)^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479789 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479828 1069 "q-185213" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479828 1071 "If $-5=\\frac{t}{4},$ then $t=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479828 1072 #uuid "063f6e3f-2db2-5183-9271-70afe486d5c2" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479828 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\frac{t}{4}=-5\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $t$ is being divided by $4.$ To isolate $t$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $4.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{4} & =-5 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{t}{4} & =4\\cdot (-5) \\\\ \\frac{4t}{4} & =-20 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}t}{\\cancel{4}} & =-20 \\\\ t & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479766 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479829 1069 "q-185357" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479829 1071 "If $x+6=5,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479829 1072 #uuid "06a538f6-9964-548c-a747-1f94df2f31bc" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479829 1074 "If we substitute $x=-1$ into the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+6 & =5 \\\\ (-1)+6 & =5 \\\\ -1+6 & =5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479823 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479830 1069 "q-82477" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479830 1071 "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2x \\\\ x-3y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479830 1072 #uuid "071d0b70-e887-5685-aa09-942aa6ae2d26" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479830 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479800 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479831 1069 "q-6233" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479831 1071 "If $-\\frac{x}{7}=-3,$ then $x=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479831 1072 #uuid "0b5cb4d7-77e4-5ac4-8439-cb36ef6f1c98" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479831 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{7} & =-3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{7}) & =(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{x}{7} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $x$ is being *divided* by $7.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $7.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $7$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{7} & =3 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{x}{7} & =7\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{7x}{7} & =21 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{7}} & =21 \\\\ x & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479812 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479832 1069 "q-161848" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479832 1071 "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\nI. The function $f$ is invertible in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\nII. The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\nIII. The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)={f}^{′}(a).$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479832 1072 #uuid "0bbe6736-f555-565a-b497-ef9dcdc55838" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479832 1074 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479825 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479833 1069 "q-65128" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479833 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y+1$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y+1$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479833 1072 #uuid "0c2a62d8-6d93-5394-bb23-b474c6edf899" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479833 1074 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479761 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479834 1069 "q-94871" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479834 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+(x{e}^{xy}+2y)\\,j$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479834 1072 #uuid "0ccfc773-296b-50db-8da8-c0588c64c45e" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479834 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+(x{e}^{xy}+2y)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t+1$ and $y=t-1$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(t-1){e}^{(t+1)(t-1)}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{(t+1)(t-1)}+2(t-1))\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\cdot 1+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+(t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2 \\\\  & =2t{e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & =2(-1){e}^{(-1)^{2}-1}+2(-1)-2 \\\\  & =-2-2-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479817 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479835 1069 "q-38880" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479835 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B}$, given that\n$\\,$ $[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479835 1072 #uuid "0f740568-e21f-5fbd-943a-06810f0bb27d" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479835 1074 "The image of $[x]_{B}$ relative to the basis $B$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479760 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479836 1069 "q-18321" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479836 1071 "Find the angle between $a=⟨1,-1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479836 1072 #uuid "0ffc2b95-6d99-59f1-9d71-68fe0c7010aa" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479836 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}})≈{108.4}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479750 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479837 1069 "q-14373" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479837 1071 "$\\frac{3+2x}{2x+3}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479837 1072 #uuid "155136a2-7453-5b52-882f-507c2e2bad0d" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479837 1074 "First, we notice that $2x+3=3+2x.$ So we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+2x}{2x+3}=\\frac{3+2x}{3+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3+2x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+2x}{3+2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(3+2x)}}{\\cancel{(3+2x)}} & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $1.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479776 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479838 1069 "q-67237" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479838 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{6x}{2t}-\\frac{3x}{t}$ if $x=-1$ and $t=3$ ." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479838 1072 #uuid "15e3ee11-29b6-56d8-a84c-a4bce1a94db1" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479838 1074 "We replace $x$ with $-1$ and $t$ with $3$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x}{2t}-\\frac{3x}{t} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(-1)}{2(3)}-\\frac{3(-1)}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{-6}{6}-\\frac{-3}{3} & = \\\\ -1-(-1) & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479744 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479839 1069 "q-4974" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479839 1071 "If $x-5=2,$ then $x=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479839 1072 #uuid "1b15f6ab-703e-5435-802f-d0e72d7b1bd2" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479839 1074 "If we substitute $x=7$ into the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =2 \\\\ 7-5 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=7.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479791 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479840 1069 "q-64695" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479840 1071 "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+100y=0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479840 1072 #uuid "217cbddd-95c6-5476-9ab1-c973151ef34b" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479840 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+100{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+100) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+100=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 10\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lambda =\\pm 10\\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation. \n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{10\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{10\\text{i}x})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x}{10}$ is not a solution." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479779 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479841 1069 "q-95074" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479841 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479841 1072 #uuid "23fc0071-5d5d-57b9-949a-5e0f696a08ef" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479841 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j+4z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1,$ $y=t-1$ and $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(t+1)\\,i+2(t-1)\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2(t+1)\\,i+2(t-1)\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (i+j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =2(t+1)\\cdot 1+2(t-1)\\cdot 1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t+2+2t-2+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479759 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479842 1069 "q-95055" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479842 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479842 1072 #uuid "2ad8b1a6-b8e1-5739-bfbc-4544e317c6e3" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479842 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}y}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{t+3}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479806 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479843 1069 "q-64691" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479843 1071 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}+y=0,$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479843 1072 #uuid "2e82d486-592d-55be-b437-74783b93d164" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479843 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the auxiliary equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$ are the (purely imaginary) roots of the auxiliary equation." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479822 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479844 1069 "q-104729" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479844 1071 "If $-8x=-56$, then $x=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479844 1072 #uuid "3418ccb3-fdc3-5d51-a5fe-8860e2e0eae1" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479844 1074 "The variable $x$ is being *multiplied* by $-8.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-8.$ So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-8.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8x & =-56 \\\\ \\frac{-8x}{-8} & =\\frac{-56}{-8} \\\\ \\frac{-8x}{-8} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-8x}{-8} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-8}x}{\\cancel{-8}} & =7 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479793 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479845 1069 "q-82452" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479845 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B},$ given that\n$\\,$ $[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479845 1072 #uuid "3570dc7e-1a01-5006-842e-6d17aa9f3a4b" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479845 1074 "The image of $[x]_{B}$ relative to the basis $B$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479762 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479846 1069 "q-100066" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479846 1071 "What is $\\frac{yz}{3xz}$ reduced to lowest terms?" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479846 1072 #uuid "4044453a-5a6d-5fd7-b8c8-665b004cb36e" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479846 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{yz}{3xz}=\\frac{y\\cdot z}{3\\cdot x\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $z$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\cdot z}{3\\cdot x\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{y\\cdot \\cancel{z}}{3\\cdot x\\cdot \\cancel{z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{y}{3x}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479815 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479847 1069 "q-71612" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479847 1071 "If $3-c=4,$ then $c=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479847 1072 #uuid "42533c96-48d8-5008-9e2c-fd9575aaa4a1" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479847 1074 "If we substitute $c=-1$ into the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-c & =4 \\\\ 3-(-1) & =4 \\\\ 3+1 & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $c=-1.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479768 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479808 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479848 1069 "q-334920" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479848 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"160px\">$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"160px\">${e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479848 1072 #uuid "462afb47-f28d-50fb-a384-b84e85e58f07" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479848 1074 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t)+3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479757 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479849 1069 "q-185509" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479849 1071 "If $\\frac{56}{x}=7,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479849 1072 #uuid "47a845f2-df50-5b7b-8443-4a0869837acd" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479849 1074 "If we substitute $x=8$ into the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{56}{x} & =7 \\\\ \\frac{56}{8} & =7 \\\\ 7 & =7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=8.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479813 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479850 1069 "q-69909" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479850 1071 "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479850 1072 #uuid "4a507c40-e9a1-5cff-8a8b-72e01c23590f" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479850 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479764 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479851 1069 "q-77997" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479851 1071 "![](images/d6/d6a750225b9912b9b4735f5dbc0f8b0a8b63fbe7616405e0e53bff31d10fc61d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above,  $i$ and $j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$, while ${i}^{′}$ and  ${j}^{′}$ are their images under the linear transformation $T.$\nWhat is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479851 1072 #uuid "4d5db12b-aca4-5679-a4bf-a23907fa0446" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479851 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n${i}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n${j}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479786 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479852 1069 "q-94874" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479852 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\sqrt{2}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y})i+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=2t\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479852 1072 #uuid "4fd8f651-0427-56fb-a0df-c03def6f1c03" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479852 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y})i+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=1-2t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1-2t)+\\frac{2t}{2t+1-2t})i+\\frac{2t}{2t+1-2t}\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(-2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2t\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\cdot 2+2t\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4t-4t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\sqrt{2}))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479767 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479853 1069 "q-160585" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479853 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479853 1072 #uuid "52539dbe-37de-5500-97cf-839689e21baa" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479853 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and $v(x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 1-(-2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (x+y)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479751 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479798 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479854 1069 "q-334927" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479854 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"100px\">$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"100px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479854 1072 #uuid "55496bb1-52a1-5f42-8a88-3b3b54310c44" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479854 1074 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479819 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479855 1069 "q-2529" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479855 1071 "If $\\frac{y}{3}=-7,$ then $y=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479855 1072 #uuid "559e6eeb-8cf0-577b-a9ee-88ad70f4ec15" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479855 1074 "In the equation, $y$ is being *divided* by $3.$ To isolate $y$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $3.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =-7 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & =3\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & =-21\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{3} & =-21 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & =-21 \\\\ y & =-21\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479758 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479856 1069 "q-161864" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479856 1071 "![](images/53/539115fd470666cb84e3de4b9b3471db6f04d0af6fd3e33897e5115361fe4e7a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=1.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\nI. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\nII. ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.87$\n\nIII. The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=3$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)}$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479856 1072 #uuid "58964444-2a46-54cf-b2ee-de1f5f5033d5" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479856 1074 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-3$ since ${f}^{′}(-3)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $y=-0.87=f(-1),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=-1,$ for example, $(-2,0),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=-1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(-1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.87.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=3=f(1),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479749 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479857 1069 "q-99873" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479857 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{p(5p-2)}{6(3p+8)}$ for $p=-3.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479857 1072 #uuid "5b344d92-93c1-5290-a0ad-6b1e4107b056" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479857 1074 "We replace $p$ with $-3$ in the above expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{p(5p-2)}{6(3p+8)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-3)(5(-3)-2)}{6(3(-3)+8)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-3)(-15-2)}{6(-9+8)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-3)(-17)}{6(-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{51}{(-6)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479745 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479858 1069 "q-71813" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479858 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x<2,\\,2<y<4\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479858 1072 #uuid "600bd5a7-d330-5937-a607-067ad2bf01ca" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479858 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,3).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479787 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479859 1069 "q-167407" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479859 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479859 1072 #uuid "608efbbd-2dcf-58ab-9644-3b94a19193a4" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479859 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2t}+\\frac{t}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479777 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479860 1069 "q-100198" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479860 1071 "${25}^{-1}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479860 1072 #uuid "61aa5cdd-68ec-5b7d-a4df-b02fb60d2719" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479860 1074 "The expression ${25}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $25.$ Hence\n\n$$\n{25}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{25}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479748 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479861 1069 "q-67242" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479861 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{z(5+2z)}{3z}?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479861 1072 #uuid "61eea428-2437-5847-999f-591f4a5a456c" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479861 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z(5+2z)}{3z}=\\frac{z\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $z$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{z}\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot \\cancel{z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5+2z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{5+2z}{3}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479803 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479862 1069 "q-77952" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479862 1071 "Given that $\\{i,j,k\\}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ and that the images of $i,$ $j,$ and $k$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨1,-4,-1⟩,$ $⟨-3,-2,5⟩,$ and $⟨1,0,0⟩,$ respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479862 1072 #uuid "659a44e7-6c36-5b88-91e1-e77f6a2823f8" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479862 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479795 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479863 1069 "q-94885" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479863 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,3t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479863 1072 #uuid "68446125-1b3e-53ed-aad0-d4e0129128a8" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479863 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{5}{1+25(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479805 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479864 1069 "q-185352" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479864 1071 "The solution to the equation $x+4=16$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479864 1072 #uuid "72233131-e693-5fea-8917-0160c71e51e0" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479864 1074 "If we substitute $x=12$ into the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =16 \\\\ 12+4 & =16 \\\\ 16 & =16\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=12.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479816 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479865 1069 "q-185208" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479865 1071 "If $4=\\frac{x}{6},$ then $x=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479865 1072 #uuid "7ce50429-066c-52cc-b521-7f9c1d989259" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479865 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{6}=4\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $x$ is being divided by $6.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $6.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6} & =4 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{x}{6} & =6\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{6x}{6} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}x}{\\cancel{6}} & =24 \\\\ x & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479818 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479866 1069 "q-43831" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479866 1071 "Given that $\\{i,j\\}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ and that the images of $i$ and $j$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨-4,3⟩$ and $⟨5,0⟩$, respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479866 1072 #uuid "7db25f7d-3356-5632-8759-eb38fc83f21e" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479866 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for this linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479754 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479867 1069 "q-171554" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479867 1071 "If $9z=63,$ then $z=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479867 1072 #uuid "837b73e9-3871-5e24-9f24-4e27d1769365" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479867 1074 "If we substitute $z=7$ into the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9z & =63 \\\\ 9\\cdot 7 & =63 \\\\ 63 & =63\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z=7.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479814 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479868 1069 "q-75369" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479868 1071 "$\\frac{12(x-1)}{15x}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479868 1072 #uuid "85ebb17c-ed44-5cf2-a5c7-233470157f33" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479868 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $12(x-1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1),$ and the $15x$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 5\\cdot x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12(x-1)}{15x}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1)}{3\\cdot 5\\cdot x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1)}{3\\cdot 5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot (x-1)}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (x-1)}{5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(x-1)}{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{4(x-1)}{5x}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479792 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479801 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479811 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479869 1069 "q-335431" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479869 1071 "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y({t}_{0})={\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y({t}_{0})=x({t}_{0})$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L9}$ follows from line $\\text{L7}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479869 1072 #uuid "87681964-b1bd-5510-b05d-db92c8b0be06" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479869 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Differentiating $y(t)={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)$ and applying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating and using the product rule}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)=x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L6}$ by ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by }{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$, $\\text{L8}$, and $\\text{L9}.$ From $\\text{L8}$ we know that\n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t)$ into\n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by $\\text{substituting }{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t)=x(t).$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479770 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479870 1069 "q-65129" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479870 1071 "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ if ${f}_{yy}(x,y)=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479870 1072 #uuid "87b5b3e0-dcda-563d-b53d-0df7f66aab12" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479870 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+(1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\,\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479802 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479871 1069 "q-175684" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479871 1071 "If $x=4,$ then $\\frac{5x-4}{x-2}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479871 1072 #uuid "87efd59d-ce0a-5957-a5fd-7fe6753d07aa" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479871 1074 "We replace $x$ with $4$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-4}{x-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(4)-4}{4-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{16}{2} & = \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479780 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479872 1069 "q-69432" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479872 1071 "What is $(-12)^{-1}?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479872 1072 #uuid "8a458e24-cc7c-5bd6-9622-f18c8346613c" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479872 1074 "The expression $(-12)^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $-12.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(-12)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479783 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479873 1069 "q-75370" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479873 1071 "$\\frac{18a}{9(a+2)}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479873 1072 #uuid "8cbe3ef2-930d-574e-83d8-95fc23d584d3" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479873 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $18a$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a,$ and the $9(a+2)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18a}{9(a+2)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $3$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot a}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot (a+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot a}{a+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a}{a+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{2a}{a+2}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479747 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479874 1069 "q-173732" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479874 1071 "If $x=4,$ then $\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{12}{5x}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479874 1072 #uuid "9225fcc6-6a47-5dc8-ba01-452e86bfb00e" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479874 1074 "We replace $x$ with $4$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{12}{5x} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}+\\frac{12}{5\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ 1+\\frac{3}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{5}+\\frac{3}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{5+3}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479746 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479875 1069 "q-140568" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479875 1071 "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479875 1072 #uuid "9369c09e-6bcc-5680-9471-b4a602dad377" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479875 1074 "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{140}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{35}})≈{75.31}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479796 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479876 1069 "q-167500" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479876 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\frac{1}{t},t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479876 1072 #uuid "99c3ab32-c332-5aab-98f3-193b3fead123" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479876 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=\\frac{1}{t},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 2t+(-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479774 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479877 1069 "q-67226" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479877 1071 "If $\\frac{k}{8}=3,$ then $k=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479877 1072 #uuid "9a04160c-cdc7-57d1-904e-82dbc1485a07" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479877 1074 "If we substitute $k=24$ into the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{8} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{24}{8} & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $k=24.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479790 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479878 1069 "q-155" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479878 1071 "$\\frac{2(x-1)}{2(x+1)}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479878 1072 #uuid "a06dc73c-225f-5c37-b7d5-563ea645e257" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479878 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $2(x-1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot (x-1),$ and the $2(x+1)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot (x+1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-1)}{2(x+1)}=\\frac{2\\cdot (x-1)}{2\\cdot (x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is only one pair of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot (x-1)}{2\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (x-1)}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)}{(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{x-1}{x+1}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479756 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479879 1069 "q-74127" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479879 1071 "Let $A(4,-1,1,0)$, $B(4,2,1,-1)$, and $C(0,-5,3,2).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠BAC.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479879 1072 #uuid "a9155dd9-42f9-5296-ab8b-8f9a62ed1901" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479879 1074 "Let $v=\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $w=\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =b-a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =c-a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta =m∠BAC$ be the angle between the vectors $v$ and $w.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{40}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14÷2}{20÷2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479788 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479880 1069 "q-167406" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479880 1071 "If $f(x,y)={e}^{x-y}$ and $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479880 1072 #uuid "aa703c37-d0d3-5fc5-8021-cece98eb14f3" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479880 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,i-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i-\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,i-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,j)\\cdot (2t\\,i-\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\cdot 2t+(-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =(2t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479794 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479881 1069 "q-71811" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479881 1071 "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {y}^{2}\\le 4\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9,\\,0\\le x\\le 3\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le y<\\sqrt{x},\\,1\\le x\\le 4\\}$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479881 1072 #uuid "aa86399c-45ea-5f74-a86a-9df5e96aefc4" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479881 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,0).$\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(4,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479809 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479882 1069 "q-75388" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479882 1071 "If $-\\frac{c}{12}=4,$ then $c=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479882 1072 #uuid "b4c0e9de-946c-5a43-9beb-5626829b00ed" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479882 1074 "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{c}{12} & =4 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{c}{12}) & =(-1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{c}{12} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $c$ is being divided by $12.$ To isolate $c$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $12.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{12} & =-4 \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{c}{12} & =12\\cdot (-4) \\\\ \\frac{12c}{12} & =-48 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}c}{\\cancel{12}} & =-48 \\\\ c & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479820 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479883 1069 "q-95053" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479883 1071 "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479883 1072 #uuid "b5969d89-d7a6-55d3-97d8-1ebc95c98dd0" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479883 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+y)\\,i+x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1$ and $y=-{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2t+2-{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2t+2-{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i-2t\\,j) \\\\  & =2t+2-{t}^{2}-2t(t+1) \\\\  & =2t+2-{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}-2t \\\\  & =2-3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479824 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479884 1069 "q-41549" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479884 1071 "$\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479884 1072 #uuid "b68074c4-93d7-57b0-8864-ec0076cbeea0" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479884 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $16$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2,$ and the $20(y-3)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2}{5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5(y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{4}{5(y-3)}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479797 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479885 1069 "q-75375" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479885 1071 "$\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479885 1072 #uuid "b735757b-2dac-511b-87bc-3911444d7136" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479885 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $3a(a-b)$ in the numerator becomes $3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b),$ and the $9(a-b)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)}=\\frac{3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b)}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(a-b)$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)} & =\\frac{3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b)}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}\\cdot a\\cdot \\cancel{(a-b)}}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{(a-b)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{a}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{a}{3}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479755 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479886 1069 "q-74108" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479886 1071 "Let $A(2,2,1,-1)$ and $B(1,0,0,3).$ Find the cosine of the angle $∠AOB$, where $O(0,0,0,0)$ is the origin." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479886 1072 #uuid "b814c1fe-b3ea-5814-8155-d4d3b5c1b767" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479886 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, let $\\theta =m∠AOB$ be the angle between the vectors $a$ and $b.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479782 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479887 1069 "q-69437" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479887 1071 "${(\\frac{7}{8})}^{-1}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479887 1072 #uuid "bfba5122-2e77-55a1-99f5-47e064f8cc14" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479887 1074 "The expression ${(\\frac{7}{8})}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $\\frac{7}{8}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{-1}=\\frac{8}{7}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479773 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479888 1069 "q-38089" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479888 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B}$, given that\n$\\,$ $[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479888 1072 #uuid "c2f444bc-6844-54b3-8db1-284a3e7b2b75" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479888 1074 "The image of $[x]_{B}$ relative to the basis $B$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479810 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479889 1069 "q-94889" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479889 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}+z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{e}^{t}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479889 1072 #uuid "c6083028-59c6-57ef-95a8-453f1847efa9" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479889 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y},x{e}^{y},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=4,$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =t\\cdot 0+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =4+{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479821 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479890 1069 "q-175906" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479890 1071 "If $x=-4$ and $y=3,$ then $\\frac{3x+2}{3}+\\frac{x+y}{2y}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479890 1072 #uuid "c7c69cd2-d2ae-54a6-bb6d-38d0dd05009e" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479890 1074 "Replacing $x$ with $-4$ and $y$ with $3$ in the expression, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{3}+\\frac{x+y}{2y} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(-4)+2}{3}+\\frac{-4+3}{2(3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{-12+2}{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{10}{3}-\\frac{1}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{20}{6}-\\frac{1}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{-20-1}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{21}{6} & = \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479765 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479891 1069 "q-101709" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479891 1071 "If $-5t=-60$, then $t=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479891 1072 #uuid "d1c0c05b-9184-5c94-9e0b-29a6b0d0aa2b" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479891 1074 "The variable $t$ is being *multiplied* by $-5.$ To isolate $t$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-5.$ So, we divide both sides of the equation by $-5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5t & =-60 \\\\ \\frac{-5t}{-5} & =\\frac{-60}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5t}{-5} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-5$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5t}{-5} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}t}{\\cancel{-5}} & =12 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479769 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479892 1069 "q-104598" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479892 1071 "${16}^{-1}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479892 1072 #uuid "d2db0e2b-c2ae-5849-b446-12d0b1a083c2" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479892 1074 "The expression ${16}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of  $16.$ Hence\n\n$$\n{16}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479804 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479893 1069 "q-101703" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479893 1071 "If $\\frac{c}{7}=-15,$ then $c=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479893 1072 #uuid "d37f3cd3-5a50-54b2-83fb-c1289723c204" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479893 1074 "In the equation, $c$ is being divided by $7.$ To isolate $c$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $7.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $7.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{7} & =-15 \\\\ 7\\cdot \\frac{c}{7} & =7\\cdot (-15) \\\\ \\frac{7c}{7} & =-105\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7c}{7} & =-105 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{7}c}{\\cancel{7}} & =-105 \\\\ c & =-105\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479778 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479894 1069 "q-41557" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479894 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{32x(5z+1)}{12xz}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479894 1072 #uuid "d8919a5c-5e3b-5274-9d2f-901afddd5e90" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479894 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $32x(5z+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1),$ and the $12xz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32x(5z+1)}{12xz}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2\\text{'s}$ and one pair of $x$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(5z+1)}{3z}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{8(5z+1)}{3z}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479771 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479895 1069 "q-71157" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479895 1071 "${(\\frac{2}{11})}^{-1}=$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479895 1072 #uuid "da68c3e8-bcc9-5b44-b44b-1f5e2fc2eb9a" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479895 1074 "The expression ${(\\frac{2}{11})}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $\\frac{2}{11}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{11})}^{-1}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479784 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479896 1069 "q-185499" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479896 1071 "If $-8n=64,$ then $n=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479896 1072 #uuid "e151b111-03ef-5f1a-9971-58474f0c89d9" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479896 1074 "If we substitute $n=-8$ into the given equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8n & =64 \\\\ -8\\cdot (-8) & =64 \\\\ 64 & =64\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $n=-8.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479775 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479897 1069 "q-175875" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479897 1071 "If $p=5,$ then $\\frac{3p}{10}+\\frac{1}{p}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479897 1072 #uuid "e26eed03-f29f-5ce3-a1e7-151a8b9035cd" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479897 1074 "We replace $p$ with $5$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3p}{10}+\\frac{1}{p}= \\\\ \\frac{3(5)}{10}+\\frac{1}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{1}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{2}{10} & = \\\\ \\frac{15+2}{10} & = \\\\ \\frac{17}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479807 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479898 1069 "q-77951" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479898 1071 "Given that $\\{i,j,k\\}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ and that the images of $i,$ $j,$ and $k$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨1,-1,3⟩,$ $⟨-4,3,5⟩,$ and $⟨0,-3,1⟩,$ respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479898 1072 #uuid "e7e40f9d-527f-556e-806c-7a796edd693d" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479898 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479781 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479899 1069 "q-95143" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479899 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479899 1072 #uuid "f5e2cc69-b41b-55a6-ba1d-f0aa44f4db50" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479899 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,i-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,j+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y={t}^{2}$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}\\,k)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479753 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479900 1069 "q-99771" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479900 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{5x+8}{3-x}$ for $x=1.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479900 1072 #uuid "fa6ce3e2-d928-5ded-b467-af49a788ffd9" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479900 1074 "We replace $x$ with $1$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+8}{3-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(1)+8}{3-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479763 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479901 1069 "q-41552" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479901 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{(3x)(7y)}{28y}.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479901 1072 #uuid "fad200a4-e2f6-57c9-aa77-2766fc482dc1" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479901 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $(3x)(7y)$ in the numerator becomes $3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y,$ and the $28y$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3x)(7y)}{28y}=\\frac{3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $7\\text{'s}$ and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{7}\\cdot x\\cdot \\cancel{y}}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{7}\\cdot \\cancel{y}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{3}{4}x.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479772 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479902 1069 "q-94880" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479902 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,j$ and $r(t)=({t}^{2}+1)\\,i+t\\,j.$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479902 1072 #uuid "fc2162e8-e3d2-5905-8722-73fb096c219a" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479902 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={t}^{2}+1$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+j)\\cdot (2t\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479903 1058 #edb/ref 17592186479907 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479903 1062 #uuid "06948950-1b74-5ba9-8396-c217d7f75455" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479903 1063 #edb/ref 17592186348068 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479904 1058 #edb/ref 17592186479905 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479904 1062 #uuid "56c91c62-cd7d-583e-8106-f750dde05e74" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479904 1063 #edb/ref 17592186479840 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479905 1058 #edb/ref 17592186479906 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479905 1062 #uuid "94fe629c-0044-5dc0-9a0f-8a48b281c0d9" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479905 1063 #edb/ref 17592186357482 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479906 1062 #uuid "d40ce1a8-b6a7-582c-8f49-39f5f17648ff" 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479906 1063 #edb/ref 17592186265886 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479907 1058 #edb/ref 17592186479904 13194139535206 17592186045417 true] [17592186479907